  "term": "Sunk cost",
  "title": "Sunk cost, explained by Ledger",
  "slug": "sunk-cost",
  "family_or_category": "economics",
  "caption": "You paid for it, so you have to use it. Or do you?\n\nA sunk cost is money already spent that no decision can recover. In this short video, Ledger meets one twice: a $60 concert ticket on a rainy night, and a company that has spent $2 million on an app nobody uses. In both cases, the money already spent should not decide what happens next\n\nGood decisions weigh only future costs and benefits. More at edlintics.com\n\n#edlintics #businessstudents #economics #finance",
  "caption_x": "Sunk cost in 40 seconds: a $60 ticket, a $2 million app, and why neither should decide what comes next. edlintics.com #edlintics",
  "first_comment": "Where have you seen a sunk cost decide something it should not have, such as a gym membership, a course or a project at work? Reply with one example. Bonus challenge: a firm has spent $300,000 on a product, finishing it costs $100,000 more, and it would bring in $80,000. Should the firm finish it? The full worked explanation is on edlintics.com",
  "maths_check": "Bonus: $80,000 future benefit < $100,000 future cost, so do not finish; the $300,000 is sunk.",
